WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.161 --> 00:00:05.651
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.651 --> 00:00:12.169
Many people would like to think
that racism is a thing of the past,

00:00:12.169 --> 00:00:18.331
but I'm here to tell you that
racism is institutionalized and

00:00:18.331 --> 00:00:22.138
very much a part of our systems today.

00:00:22.138 --> 00:00:27.001
Prior to business school I spent a lot of
my spare time advising small businesses.

00:00:27.001 --> 00:00:31.825
And during that time I saw time and
time again that they could be two

00:00:31.825 --> 00:00:36.563
businesses with virtually the same
exact profile on paper, but

00:00:36.563 --> 00:00:41.302
experience totally different
outcomes when the only thing that

00:00:41.302 --> 00:00:45.445
separated the two was the color
of their owner's skin.

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:49.058
Every time I go back home to my
hometown of Baltimore, Maryland,

00:00:49.058 --> 00:00:50.846
I'm known as the business guy.

00:00:50.846 --> 00:00:53.727
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean, I've done taxes for

00:00:53.727 --> 00:00:58.494
mobile retailers, marketing plans for
bus companies, I've read tons and

00:00:58.494 --> 00:01:02.011
tons of business plans,
I've even managed property.

00:01:02.011 --> 00:01:06.693
All while working full time in
Washington DC as a regulatory,

00:01:06.693 --> 00:01:10.121
compliance and cyber security consultant.

00:01:10.121 --> 00:01:15.797
But one day I got a call from the daycare
center that I went to while growing

00:01:15.797 --> 00:01:22.234
up in Baltimore, asking for my help with
their small business loan application.

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:23.586
I mean, I went to this center.

00:01:23.586 --> 00:01:28.742
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My brother went there,

00:01:28.742 --> 00:01:30.911
my mother had worked there.

00:01:30.911 --> 00:01:35.658
In over 22 years this center had
touched the lives of thousands of

00:01:35.658 --> 00:01:40.171
people within our community and
had a huge impact on my family.

00:01:40.171 --> 00:01:44.097
So when I went to visit them I
saw that they have 46 children

00:01:44.097 --> 00:01:48.973
currently enrolled in their program,
eight full-time employees, and

00:01:48.973 --> 00:01:53.948
a long, long wait list of families just
hoping to enroll in their program.

00:01:53.948 --> 00:01:58.415
I noticed that, on paper,
this was a solid business opportunity.

00:01:58.415 --> 00:02:04.350
With 22 years of track record of positive
cash flows, and also profitability.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.773
So I prepared their business plan and
their application, and

00:02:08.773 --> 00:02:14.225
I went to a bank, and after few meetings,
our loan application was denied.

00:02:14.225 --> 00:02:18.313
We moved forward and
we went to a second bank, but

00:02:18.313 --> 00:02:23.720
after few meetings, again,
our loan application was denied.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.111
So I asked a few questions and
re-revisited and

00:02:27.111 --> 00:02:31.794
revised our materials, and
we presented an even stronger case.

00:02:31.794 --> 00:02:36.098
And we went to a third bank, and again,

00:02:36.098 --> 00:02:39.890
our loan application was denied.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:44.734
So I felt confused, I felt helpless.

00:02:44.734 --> 00:02:49.187
Why couldn't the banks
acknowledge the value that was

00:02:49.187 --> 00:02:53.654
clearly on the paper,
a solid business opportunity?

00:02:53.654 --> 00:02:57.444
According to a Baltimore
economic development group,

00:02:57.444 --> 00:03:02.201
black entrepreneurs are denied small
business loans up to three times

00:03:02.201 --> 00:03:04.878
more often than white entrepreneurs.

00:03:04.878 --> 00:03:07.841
So I asked myself a question.

00:03:07.841 --> 00:03:12.845
Why can't black people
get loans in Baltimore?

00:03:12.845 --> 00:03:14.387
And so I Googled it.

00:03:14.387 --> 00:03:20.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.440
I spent some time scrolling
through the results.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.369
And after a few minutes I saw a map.

00:03:27.369 --> 00:03:30.196
And honestly, I'll never forget the map.

00:03:30.196 --> 00:03:35.434
So if I step back in time and
tell you about the 1930s, during that time

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:41.534
the government had amazing ideas, like
the 30 year fixed interest rate mortgage.

00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:47.233
The government also created the Home
Owners Loan Cooperation, the HOLC,

00:03:47.233 --> 00:03:52.308
which was designed to evaluate
239 cities across the country.

00:03:52.308 --> 00:03:57.714
And literally drew lines on
maps categorizing them green,

00:03:57.714 --> 00:04:00.101
blue, yellow, and red.

00:04:00.101 --> 00:04:06.836
Green meant the rich upper income,
White people areas.

00:04:06.836 --> 00:04:11.051
Blue meant middle class white people.

00:04:11.051 --> 00:04:14.585
Yellow meant poor white people.

00:04:14.585 --> 00:04:21.688
And then black, I said that wrong, red,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:21.688 --> 00:04:24.827
&gt;&gt; Red was labeled hazardous on the map.

00:04:24.827 --> 00:04:28.430
And red meant black.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.845
Do we see the difference here?

00:04:30.845 --> 00:04:35.613
The government literally created
a system where they categorized

00:04:35.613 --> 00:04:40.635
the White people by socioeconomic class,
and then created a separate

00:04:40.635 --> 00:04:45.756
catch-all bucket of his own for
Black people and marked it hazardous.

00:04:45.756 --> 00:04:52.200
Red line maps, like this old map
from the 1930s in Baltimore,

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.473
institutionalized racism in my hometown.

00:04:56.473 --> 00:05:01.622
And a study as recent
as 2017 found that 74%,

00:05:01.622 --> 00:05:09.462
74% of areas that were marked red back
in the 1930s are today still low and

00:05:09.462 --> 00:05:15.315
moderate income in almost
entirely majority black today,

00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:20.580
while 91%,
91% of areas that were marked green

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.814
over 80 years ago are upper income and
majority white today.

00:05:29.713 --> 00:05:35.094
So redlining like this has
really had an impact on so

00:05:35.094 --> 00:05:40.853
many communities, and frankly,
it makes a mockery of

00:05:40.853 --> 00:05:46.127
our ideals of the American
dream in this country.

00:05:46.127 --> 00:05:50.887
In 1977, the government created
the Community Reinvestment Act,

00:05:50.887 --> 00:05:55.411
which is a law that federally mandated
banks to devote a portion of its

00:05:55.411 --> 00:05:59.000
loan portfolio investments and
services to the low and

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.153
moderate income communities in
which they were operating in.

00:06:03.153 --> 00:06:06.951
But too many neighborhoods,
too many people, and

00:06:06.951 --> 00:06:13.239
too many businesses are just not seeing
those loans, investments, and services.

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:17.603
Just come, travel to Maryland with me and
you can see it for yourself.

00:06:17.603 --> 00:06:21.414
It's going on right in
the front of our eyes and

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:25.436
we are not doing anything
to make change happen.

00:06:25.436 --> 00:06:30.591
The Community Reinvestment Act
was meant to end redlining, but

00:06:30.591 --> 00:06:35.747
racism has been institutionalized
in our healthcare systems,

00:06:35.747 --> 00:06:41.778
education systems, banking, housing,
and criminal justice systems.

00:06:41.778 --> 00:06:48.751
And frankly, it makes a mockery of
our ideals of the American dream.

00:06:50.816 --> 00:06:56.076
There are billions and
billions of dollars of high quality

00:06:56.076 --> 00:07:02.096
investment opportunities that
are still being left on the table.

00:07:02.096 --> 00:07:07.820
Black Americans have $1.2
trillion of buying power and

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:12.284
incredibly innovative ideas for
products and

00:07:12.284 --> 00:07:17.905
businesses that just need better,
that deserve better.

00:07:17.905 --> 00:07:24.672
But we continue to leave those high
quality opportunities on the table.

00:07:24.672 --> 00:07:27.702
We haven't erased the red
lines of the past,

00:07:27.702 --> 00:07:32.261
we just continue to limit our
country's full economic potential.

00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:40.152
Is this the type of country
we want to live in,

00:07:40.152 --> 00:07:47.679
to raise our families in,
to leave for our children, really?

00:07:47.679 --> 00:07:52.688
Is it okay that your zip code
is a better predictor of

00:07:52.688 --> 00:07:58.169
your health outcomes than
your genetic code, really?

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:02.831
Is it okay that three out of
four neighborhoods that were

00:08:02.831 --> 00:08:07.787
marked red over 80 years ago
are the majority of our low and

00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:11.770
moderate income communities today, really?

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:16.513
Is it okay that even on this
very past weekend, in 2019,

00:08:16.513 --> 00:08:22.469
a very nice woman still asks me, what is
it like to be a black man in business?

00:08:22.469 --> 00:08:25.520
Because it must be really, really hard.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.128
And you know what?

00:08:27.128 --> 00:08:32.653
She's right, it's hard, because
collectively we haven't been questioning

00:08:32.653 --> 00:08:37.542
our ideals and perspectives on race,
class, and the American dream.

00:08:37.542 --> 00:08:41.365
Collectively we haven't been
questioning our local banks and

00:08:41.365 --> 00:08:45.475
asking them what are they doing to
provide loans and investments and

00:08:45.475 --> 00:08:50.041
services into the low income communities
in which they're operating in.

00:08:50.041 --> 00:08:53.392
But today, you can help.

00:08:53.392 --> 00:08:56.500
And right now we can
all make a difference.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.485
I recently launched a website
called grantbridge.org,

00:09:00.485 --> 00:09:05.444
where you can go and you can nominate
a business that may be underserved so

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:10.254
we can better connect them with their
local banks in their community.

00:09:10.254 --> 00:09:16.552
Today, you can change the paradigm and
shift the way that we think about risk,

00:09:16.552 --> 00:09:22.565
and move away from these old school
racially charged color coded systems,

00:09:22.565 --> 00:09:25.730
because collectively we need better.

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:28.714
Collectively, we all deserve better.

00:09:28.714 --> 00:09:33.196
There are billions and
billions of dollars of high

00:09:33.196 --> 00:09:37.476
quality value that's
being left on the table.

00:09:37.476 --> 00:09:41.487
Billions of dollars of
opportunity that just so

00:09:41.487 --> 00:09:45.307
happens to be located
in black communities.

00:09:45.307 --> 00:09:50.256
Today, it's time to redesign
our future for everyone,

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:56.133
including those 46 children,
those 8 workers, and that long,

00:09:56.133 --> 00:10:01.102
long wait list of families
from my daycare in Baltimore.

00:10:03.469 --> 00:10:04.125
Thank you.

00:10:04.125 --> 00:10:07.255
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:07.255 --> 00:10:11.775
[MUSIC]

